cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "chess-lab: falsifiable analyses of large public chess corpora"
message: "If you use these findings or methods, please cite this repository."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Andrew
    affiliation: "independent"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-12"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - chess
  - stockfish
  - elo
  - reproducibility
  - falsification
abstract: >-
  Eleven independent analyses of 190,967 public chess games (a 2017 lichess.org
  scrape and a 1998-2026 sample of The Week In Chess tournament issues), using
  Stockfish 16 at a fixed node budget as ground truth. Covers blunder rate
  versus thinking time, White's first-move advantage over time, whether
  engine-approved openings win more, rating inflation measured both
  cross-sectionally and within players' careers, and why lost positions are
  saved far more often online than over the board (resignation norms, not
  skill). Includes a falsification track that attempts to break the other
  tracks, specification curves over every defensible analytic choice, and a
  stated minimum detectable effect for every null result. Two claims were
  retracted before release and both retractions are enforced by tests.
